' BRAIN GAMES I
' Five puzzles from the "Brain Games" chapter of Tim Hartnell's Giant
' Book of Computer Games (Ballantine Books, 1984; Interface Publications,
' 1983), for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' FASTERMIND: crack a four-digit code (digits 1 to 9) in eight goes. Each
' black key peg is a right digit in the right place, each white one a
' right digit in the wrong place.
' IDAHO STARS: every line of the star adds up to the same total. Some
' numbers are hidden; name a number and every place it hides is shown.
' IDAHO SQUARES: the same with a magic square: rows, columns and
' diagonals share one total; four numbers are hidden.
' EXECUTIONER: guess the Executioner's word letter by letter. A word of N
' letters gives INT(N + N/2 + .5) chances; a wrong letter costs one, a
' right one gives one back for each place it fills.
' CUBIST: a flat colour cube. The computer makes ten twists; each twist
' turns a block of four squares clockwise. Put it back.
'
' Controls (either joystick): FASTERMIND: left and right pick a peg, up
' and down change its digit, the button tries the code. STARS and
' SQUARES: up and down change the number by 1, left and right by 5, the
' button names it. EXECUTIONER: the stick moves over the alphabet, the
' button picks a letter. CUBIST: the stick moves the twist point, the
' button twists. The second button leaves. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - FASTERMIND: the book says the digits are never repeated, but its
'   listing (and its own screen, "Digits can be repeated") lets them
'   repeat; the listing is followed. Each new guess starts as a copy of
'   the last one.
' - IDAHO STARS: the listing restarts when a number is 0; here it also
'   starts over when one is below 0, so every number is 1 or more. The
'   three hidden places may fall twice on one place, as in the book.
' - IDAHO SQUARES: the listing only checks four of its numbers; here a
'   square with a number below 1 is made again.
' - EXECUTIONER: a letter already tried is refused, at no cost (the
'   book charges a chance). The gallows is drawn in eleven parts as the
'   chances run out.
' - CUBIST: the squares are coloured; the twist point is chosen with the
'   stick instead of typing 2-12.
' - New pictures (pegs, the star, the square, the gallows, the cube),
'   new music (Thinking Cap) and sounds.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; one sprite (the
' pointer); every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.

